window.docContents[192].resolve({"/Foundations/Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations--Classical___-weak___-and-well-posed-problems":{"contents":"\n\nThe routes in the figure share these operators, but require different evidence\nabout a proposed solution. A classical calculation supplies pointwise\nidentities; a weak formulation pairs the function with test functions; an\nexistence proof constructs an object satisfying the chosen formulation.\nIntegration by parts relates classical and weak formulations; regularity\ncan recover classical derivatives from a weak solution.\n\nWell-posedness asks a further question about a whole family of problems.\nFor each allowed datum, does a solution exist, is it unique in the chosen\nclass, and do small changes in the data produce small changes in the solution?\nThe word “small” needs a topology on both spaces. The\nwell-posedness definition is\nintroduced through a Dirichlet problem on an interval, where all three\nrequirements are visible in one calculation. Regularity asks how many\nderivatives that solution has; it is a separate conclusion.\n\nFor differentiable initial data  and constant velocity , transport\nhas the classical solution . The chain rule gives\n and , so\n; substituting  gives the initial datum.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Operators, solution concepts, and weak formulations","header":"3.1.1. Classical, weak, and well-posed problems","id":"/Foundations/Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations--Classical___-weak___-and-well-posed-problems"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Second-Variations-and-Dirichlet-Energy--Quadratic-Energies":{"contents":"For a symmetric bounded bilinear form  and continuous load , the\nenergysatisfies the exact expansion\n\nExpand both bilinear arguments and the linear load, use symmetry on the two\nmixed terms, and collect coefficients.\n\nThe expansion is an exact Taylor formula: the term linear in  is\n, the quadratic term is , and there is no\nhigher-order remainder. The Fréchet derivative and the second variation can\ntherefore be read off directly.\n\nThe Fréchet derivative of the quadratic energy at  isor equivalently  as a continuous linear functional.\n\nMathlib's bilinear derivative rule differentiates B(u,u). Symmetry combines\nthe two resulting terms, while the load differentiates to itself.\n\nAt a local minimizer the derivative vanishes by Fermat's theorem, and the\nderivative formula turns that vanishing into an identity in every direction.\nBecause the energy is quadratic in , no separate differentiability\nhypothesis is needed here, in contrast to the semilinear energies, where\ndifferentiability of the potential has to be supplied before a minimizer can\nbe called a weak solution.\n\nEvery unconstrained local minimizer satisfies the weak Euler equation\n\nFermat's theorem makes the derivative vanish at a local minimum. Substituting\nthe derivative formula and evaluating the zero functional gives the weak\nequation.\n\nThe Euler equation used only the first derivative. The second derivative is\nwhere the quadratic structure pays off: because  depends affinely on\n, the Hessian does not depend on the base point at all, and the\nsecond-variation test can be evaluated without knowing the minimizer.\n\nThe second Fréchet derivative of the quadratic energy is independent of the\nbase point:\n\nThe first-variation map is the affine map ; its derivative\nis therefore the continuous linear map B.\n\nCombining the abstract second-order necessary condition with the base-point\nindependence of the Hessian gives a condition on  alone: a quadratic\nenergy can have a local minimizer only if  is positive semidefinite.\nCoercive forms satisfy this automatically.\n\nAt a local minimum, the second variation obeys the necessary condition\n\nIdentify the Hessian of the quadratic energy with B, then apply the abstract\ndiagonal nonnegativity theorem at the local minimum.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009Second Variations and Dirichlet Energy","header":"9.3.2.3. Quadratic Energies","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Second-Variations-and-Dirichlet-Energy--Quadratic-Energies"}});